## 4. Pattern Matching (`match`)

Think of this as a "switch on steroids." It evaluates a value against various patterns.

### Standard Usage

```scala
val day = i match
  case 0 => "Sunday"
  case 1 => "Monday"
  case 2 => "Tuesday"
  case 3 => "Wednesday"
  case 4 => "Thursday"
  case 5 => "Friday"
  case 6 => "Saturday"
  case _ => "invalid day"   // _ is the catch-all (default)

```

### Multiple Cases and Guards (`if`)

```scala
// Multiple values in one line using |
val evenOrOdd = i match
  case 1 | 3 | 5 | 7 | 9 => println("odd")
  case 2 | 4 | 6 | 8 | 10 => println("even")
  case _ => println("some other number")

// Match with conditional guards
val num = i match
  case 1 => println("one, a lonely number")
  case x if x == 2 || x == 3 => println("two’s company, three’s a crowd")
  case x if x > 3 => println("4+, that’s a party")
  case _ => println("i’m guessing your number is zero or less")

```

